\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Frequency and class}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{In the Morris–Lecar model the column decides the frequency.}

\p{\citet{Figure 3.6b}{ermentrout2010mathematical} shows the
saddle-node on an invariant circle: the frequency falls to zero
as the current approaches the fold.}

\p{\citet{Figure 3.4b}{ermentrout2010mathematical} shows the Hopf
column: the frequency stays between #{7} and #{16} Hz and
does not fall to zero at the bifurcation.}

\p{\citet{§3.4.3}{ermentrout2010mathematical} is the third column.
Onset there is a homoclinic orbit to a saddle, and the frequency
again falls to zero.}

\p{\citet{§3.4.4, p. 62}{ermentrout2010mathematical} names
Class I and Class II. Class I fires at an arbitrarily low
frequency, after a long latency, through the saddle-node on
an invariant circle. Class II has a positive minimal frequency
and matches the Hopf bifurcation.
\citet{§7.1.3, p. 222}{izhikevich2007dynamical} uses Class 1
for that circle and Class 2 for the other three bifurcations:
the fold
off the circle, and the subcritical or the supercritical
Andronov–Hopf bifurcation. After the off-circle fold, the
state goes to a periodic orbit that already exists and already
has a positive frequency, so that fold is Class 2.}

\p{\citet{Figure 1.12, p. 12}{izhikevich2007dynamical} draws
the 4 bifurcations from rest to spiking.
\citet{Figure 7.14, p. 229}{izhikevich2007dynamical} sorts
them by two features. A complex pair at rest makes a
resonator, and its absence makes an integrator. Rest that
coexists with spiking is the bistable case. At rest in the
Hopf column the equilibrium is a [focus](fnip-0035), as
\ref{fnip-0037} records, so that rest is a resonator.}

